AB  - Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) has brought a potent way of supplementing the factual accuracy of large language model (LLM) responses through external knowledge sources. Nevertheless, the conventional RAG pipelines tend to hide as black boxes, with not much information on how answers are produced, checked and justified through the retrieved content. We presented Explain-RAG, an explainable RAG framework that strives to improve trustworthiness and interpretability in question answering systems in the work. Its structure combines three fundamental elements: (1) Chain-ofThought (CoT) reasoning where the model articulates the step-by-step rationale of its logic prior to providing a final answer; (2) Natural Language Inference (NLI) validation, which checks to ensure that the presented evidence may clearly support the given answer that might include an entailment label and a confidence score; (3) Source Attribution that measures the contribution of each retrieved document to the generated response. Unison of these elements offers verifiable, readable, and understandable responses. Explain-RAG is domain-independent, flexible, and not a black-box generation system, means a more transparent solution to black-box generation system. We found that it makes both explainability and factual trust better without any domainspecific fine-tuning.
