AB  - In the agricultural domain, farmers and researchers depend on accurate, context-aware information to make timely decisions. Search engines often miss similar links among agricultural entities, and the results they provide are not very useful. To address this gap, we built a graph-based Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system for agricultural question answering. Our approach combines the structural Neo4j knowledge graph with the reasoning power of large language models. We designed a generative pipeline that automatically extracts entities and relationships from unstructured agricultural text such as reports on rice blast disease or pest overrunning in wheat and uses them to build the graph. Using LangChain, we implement dynamic “text-to-cypher” translation, so that users can query the database in plain language without any technical knowledge. Once the system retrieves relevant graph data, Google Gemini interprets the results and generates clear, context-aware answers. For example, when asked “What pesticides are effective against aphids in mustard crops?”, the system retrieves the correct relationships from the graph and produces a brief, farmer-friendly response. In preliminary tests, this model framework reduced hallucinated answers by about $\mathbf{2 0 - 2 5 \%}$ compared to standalone LLM outputs and improved retrieval precision. These results suggest that GraphRAG can serve as a reliable decision support tool for the agricultural sector, helping farmers and researchers cut through information overload and act on trustworthy insights. GitHub Repository
