AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has significantly improved question-answering systems; however, conventional RAG frameworks struggle to retrieve legally accurate information from evolving government policies because they treat legal documents as independent entities and fail to model relationships between laws and their amendments. Consequently, existing legal question-answering systems often produce outdated responses, overlook legislative amendments, and generate hallucinated information. To address these limitations, this paper proposes LexPulse, a relationship-aware RAG framework for e-governance policy retrieval that models amendment relationships using metadata-based document links. The proposed framework integrates hybrid retrieval using BM25, ChromaDB, and Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF), together with a metadata-based relationship resolver for amendment-aware query expansion. In addition, automatic context tagging distinguishes active and superseded provisions, while an adaptive LLM fallback mechanism handles out-of-domain queries using the language model’s parametric knowledge. Experimental evaluation on 51 legal questions and 18 amendment-related benchmark questions achieved accuracies of 70.6% and 72.2%, respectively. These results indicate improved legal-policy retrieval compared with conventional RAG.
