AB  - 3D scene understanding is fundamental to embodied AI, and recent 3D Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have improved visual comprehension in complex environments. However, their appearance-centric visual encoders and weakly coupled vision-instruction architecture jointly lead to predictions that are accurate in appearance yet unreliable in spatial reasoning. In this paper, we propose $V^{3}$-LLM, a generalist 3D MLLM that integrates an appearance-spatial dual-perspective vision perception system with an intent-aware vision-guided language refinement mechanism, enabling the model to adaptively reconcile appearance information and spatial visual cues according to task requirements. Specifically, the dual vision memory system encodes instance-level appearance attributes while simultaneously capturing spatially enhanced visual features, producing a comprehensive 3D scene representation. Building upon these representations, the intent-aware vision refinement LLM (IVR-LLM) employs a staged information injection schedule that first processes appearance and instruction tokens through standard decoder layers to form preliminary hidden representations, then iteratively injects intent-aware spatial preferences at later decoding stages, enabling the model to dynamically integrate appearance and spatial cues for scene reasoning. Considering the multi-granular alignment required in 3D scene descriptions, we introduce a hierarchical alignment loss that enforces consistency from token-level attributes to sentence-level spatial relations, leading to more coherent and semantically reliable scene descriptions. Extensive experiments on 3D dense captioning, visual question answering, and visual grounding benchmarks demonstrate the superiority of $V^{3}$-LLM.
