AB  - Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown strong performance in general visual understanding, yet they remain limited in complex 3D visual question answering tasks that require explicit object grounding, spatial relation modeling, and occlusion-aware reasoning. To address this challenge, we propose a modular segmentation-enhanced reasoning framework that integrates a Large Language Model (LLM), Grounded-Segment Anything Model(SAM), and an MLLM for Open3DVQA. The framework consists of three stages: (1) structured semantic parsing, in which an LLM converts naturallanguage questions into compact object-centric prompts to improve grounding reliability; (2) explicit visual grounding and segmentation, where Grounding DIstillation with NO labels(DINO) and SAM generate object labels, bounding boxes, confidence scores, and pixel-level masks; and (3) segmentation-guided multimodal reasoning, in which these structured visual cues are injected into the MLLM together with the original image and question. Experiments on Open3DVQA show that the proposed prompting strategy improves segmentation success from below 90% with raw questions to 97.35%, while segmentation-enhanced inference increases overall answer accuracy from 47.1% to 48.9%, improves qualitative reasoning from 59.3% to 61.6%, and reduces the refusal rate from 4.48% to 0.28%. The results indicate that explicit segmentation provides a practical and interpretable scaffold for multi-modal reasoning, offering a fluently low-training-cost pathway for improving MLLM performance in spatial perception tasks. At the same time, limited gains on quantitative questions suggest that segmentation alone is insufficient for full 3D reasoning, highlighting the need for future integration of geometric and depth-aware representations.
