AB  - The rapid evolution of Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) has made it difficult to organize and compare model architectures under a unified framework. Automatic taxonomy construction (ATC) is a promising approach for algorithmically organizing knowledge domains, but existing frameworks struggle with the structural complexities of ANNs. To address this, we introduce Neural Network Classification Automatic Taxonomy System (N-CATS), a structured, ontology-driven framework for ATC that enables comparison across diverse ANN topologies. N-CATS extracts contextual information from semi-structured sources, including academic publications, source code, and serialized formats such as ONNX to populate an ontology schema-based on ANNETT-O. The system leverages Large Language Models (LLM) with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to support the ontology population and organize extracted information in a graph database, enabling on-demand categorization and comparison of ANNs. To demonstrate feasibility, we developed a web application that provides ontology visualization, proof-of-concept taxonomy generation, and a chatbot using GraphRAG and Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers for intelligent querying. We compare N-CATS with existing neural network ontologies such as FAIRnets, Artificial Intelligence Ontology (AIO), and ANNETT-O. Finally, we outline advantages and limitations, demonstrating a proof-of-concept knowledge system that facilitates more efficient discovery and comparison of deep learning models as a tool for researchers.
