AB  - Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems have enhanced the performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) by effectively addressing challenges such as hallucinations and generating irrelevant responses. However, integrating the RAG technique into LLMs introduces privacy risks and potential data leakage issues from the retrieval pipeline. Indirect Prompt Injections (IPIs) occur when hidden instructions inside retrieved documents slip into a model’s context as trusted evidence, exploiting weak guardrails in RAG systems and leading to unauthorized data leaks, ambiguous outcomes, and policy overrides. Existing defenses rely on brittle delimiter heuristics or limited retriever adjustments, leaving RAG systems vulnerable to adversarial directives blended seamlessly into retrieved content.In this paper, we present RIPE-II, a corpus-level benchmark that evaluates IPIs under a realistic content-poisoning threat model. RIPE-II constructs delimiter-free, position-aware poisoned corpora across multiple domains and includes both textual and visual injections. Our design explicitly models mid-document placement, chunking, truncation, and cross-component propagation, reflecting how enterprise RAG systems ingest and process content. We introduce a two-stage evaluation framework that separates retrieval exposure from generation-time compliance and distinguishes embedding-based similarity from semantic override using a calibrated LLM-based judge. Across biomedical, financial, scientific, open-domain question answering, including multi-hop reasoning and web passage retrieval settings, we find that poisoned documents can reach the final model context in up to 97% of queries and rank first in up to 94% of cases, showing that retriever and reranker choices substantially shape RAG security exposure. We further show that cosine-based attack scoring can underreport semantic compromise by more than 5×, demonstrating that lexical or embedding similarity alone is insufficient for evaluating indirect prompt injection risk.
ER  - 

