AB  - As unstructured and semi-structured data proliferate in digital platforms, the need for intelligent information retrieval systems that can provide accurate, context-aware, and explainable results is growing. Conventional information retrieval methods have limited success in handling semantic relationships and domain knowledge, and do not perform well in complex queries. To overcome these drawbacks, this paper presents a Knowledge-Augmented Language Model (KALM) framework which combines LLM and structured knowledge resources like knowledge graphs, ontologies, and external databases. The proposed approach integrates the three techniques of processing queries and ranking documents: contextual reasoning, entity linking and semantic enrichment, to increase the effectiveness of the retrieval. The architecture is designed as a multi-layered pipeline that comprises of query understanding, knowledge augmentation, hybrid retrieval, and response generation modules. It uses transformer-based language models along with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods to dynamically integrate relevant external knowledge. Moreover, attention mechanisms and graph-based embeddings are used to enhance the semantic matching of queries to documents. The evaluation is done experimentally on some benchmark datasets and reported by precision, recall, F1-score and Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR). Results show that the proposed KALM framework can significantly outperform the retrieval baselines on the accuracy, contextual relevance, and robustness on various domains. The study emphasizes the need to combine symbolic knowledge with neural language models to address the shortcomings of data-driven models. The proposed framework provides scalable and flexible solutions in the applications such as search engine, question answering system, digital assistant, enterprise knowledge management, etc. Future research involves further optimizing real-time knowledge integration, optimizing computational costs, and increasing explainability in knowledge-driven retrieval systems.
