AB  - Semantic communications aim to transmit task-relevant meaning instead of raw data, enabling bandwidth reduction under non-ideal channel conditions, which is a critical requirement across bandwidth-constrained and noise-impaired systems, such as satellite links and resource-limited wireless networks. This paper proposes a task-adaptive semantic communication framework for visual question answering (VQA) over bandwidth-constrained additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) channels. The proposed system achieves 9× compression by transmitting a compact 16k-dimensional semantic map instead of 150k-dimensional raw image data, while maintaining task performance under severe channel noise. The proposed method contains two key contributions: (i) a multi-SNR encoder training strategy that improves low-SNR VQA accuracy by 6.3% at −20 dB compared to training under ideal channel conditions, and (ii) a semantic judge feedback mechanism using REINFORCE policy gradients, where embedding-based judges (GTE-Qwen2-7B, SBERT, BERT, and GPT-2) yield 1.9–2.1% accuracy gains over a supervised cross-entropy baseline. Experiments on the VQAv2 dataset with a ResNet-50 semantic encoder demonstrate that text-based semantic-similarity judges outperform both heuristic exact-match feedback and vision-based judges, confirming that text-grounded reward signals are better suited for bandwidth-constrained semantic VQA under realistic noisy-channel conditions.
