AB  - In recent years, table reasoning has attracted significant attention, with extensive studies that leverage the advantages of Large Language Models (LLMs) and their multi-step prompting paradigm. Existing approaches manage to produce high-quality reasoning procedures through the inspirations of operation decomposition and tool utilization. However, they place less emphasis on explicit understanding of the tabular data and pose potential risks of chaotic logical lines. We hold that robust table reasoning fundamentally depends upon a thorough comprehension of table metadata. To this end, we adopt the perspective of Explicit Meta Enhancement (EME) by building a structured semantic scaffold with explicit, well-formatted contextual knowledge for LLM-based table reasoning. This augmentation provides more comprehensible textual grounding, improving its understanding of the table schema and reducing the uncertainty in downstream reasoning. Finally, we conduct comprehensive experimental evaluations over a public table reasoning benchmark. The results fully demonstrate the effectiveness of EME in table understanding, especially in reasoning tasks with higher difficulty.
