AB  - The growing complexity of modern wireless networks motivates the need for intelligent and automated approaches for network configuration and management. This paper proposes a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)-enhanced Artificial Intelligence (AI) pipeline for translating high-level user intents into low-level 5G network configurations. Existing RAG and fine-tuning approaches for AI-assisted telecommunication systems are surveyed, revealing a critical gap: most prior work focuses on question answering rather than actionable configuration generation. To address this gap, a conceptual architecture and a proof-of-concept implementation are presented, comprising a web-based frontend, a Flask Application Programming Interface (API), the Qdrant vector database for a RAG-system, lightweight Large Language Models (LLMs) such as Phi-2 and Qwen-3-8B-Instruct, a verification component, and a time-series database. Finally, the system evaluates 50 prompts, assessing the syntactic correctness and semantic accuracy of generated configurations against a ground truth, thereby demonstrating the fundamental feasibility of LLM-driven intent-to-configuration translation in 5G networks.
