AB  - Population aging increases the need for scalable mental-health support tailored to older adults, yet LLM-based chatbots may provide non-specialized or unsafe guidance in sensitive situations such as aggressive venting or nostalgia-driven distress. We present an interpretable therapy-informed dialogue chatbot for older adults, guided by Hierarchical Linguistic Feature Inference and Social Buffering. The system combines retrieval-augmented generation with an inference-based planner that maps six clinically motivated linguistic dimensions (Time Focus, Cognitive Complexity, Emotional Tone, Social Orientation, Repetitive Patterns, and Absolutist Language) to a quantified therapy ratio vector across four therapeutic schools: CBT, PCT, SFBT, and RT. A Hierarchical Decision Algorithm (HDA) first performs cognitive screening to avoid high-load interventions, then activates a Social Buffer mechanism when rigidity markers (e.g., absolutist expressions) are detected, prioritizing emotional validation before further guidance. Persona-based simulation results show appropriate buffering behavior (higher PCT/RT for aggression) and reminiscence-oriented support for loneliness, offering a transparent alternative to end-to-end black-box dialogue systems.
