AB  - Equitable access to emergency services is a core requirement for urban resilience, yet population-aware spatial audits typically demand expert GIS workflows. We present SiagaJakarta, a reproducible and automated end-to-end spatial analysis system for Jakarta through a Telegram chatbot. The system follows a multi-agent design-LLM Orchestrator, OSM Facility Retrieval, Boundary Retrieval, Population Retrieval, and Analysis & Reporting–that translates natural-language prompts into population-normalized adequacy maps and narratives per sub-district (kecamatan). We evaluate the system on $N=180$ prompts (44 sub-districts $\times 4$ services plus synonym variants). Our contributions are: (i) a practical multi-agent architecture that unifies LLM, and geospatial analysis; (ii) a Jakarta case study producing static maps and narratives per sub-district; (iii) a multi-metric evaluation suited to governance; and (iv) a reproducible blueprint that cities can adapt with minimal coding. In our experiment, SiagaJakarta processed 100% of the 180 queries (pipeline success 100%), found facilities in 96.1% of cases, and exported static basemap figures for 100% of runs. Facility retrieval results were deterministic and consistent across repeated queries. Responses were fast (median latency $=9.17 ~\mathrm{s}$; mean $=16.12 ~\mathrm{s}; \mathbf{p 9 0}=20.42 ~\mathrm{s}; \mathbf{p 9 5}=79.39 ~\mathrm{s}$). The pipeline surfaced actionable gaps (e.g., low fire-station adequacy in industrial districts) and generated consistent cartography with embedded textual insights. These results indicate that agentic GeoAI can make population-aware spatial intelligence usable by non-experts and provide a scalable foundation for policy-oriented deployment.
