AB  - This paper introduces a conversational chatbot that closes this divide by coupling YOLOv8-based object detection, Tesseract driven optical character recognition, and Google Gemini dialogue generation inside one unified framework. Rather than requiring costly end-to-end vision-language training, the architecture constructs a structured textual context from the outputs of both vision modules and feeds it to a large language model, thereby enabling zero-shot, multi-turn conversations about arbitrary photographs with no task-specific fine-tuning. Users upload an image through a Flask web interface and immediately begin asking questions; the chatbot draws on both detected objects and extracted text to compose grounded answers. Experiments on 150 images spanning object-heavy scenes, text-dense documents, and mixed-content photographs yielded 92.5% detection accuracy (mAP@0.5), 89% character-level OCR precision, and end-to-end latency below two seconds. The system proves useful for visu-ally impaired individuals seeking interactive scene descriptions, students exploring labelled diagrams, and office staff querying scanned receipts. Because raw image pixels never leave the local device only a short text summary is sent to the cloud model the design also addresses practical privacy requirements that fully cloud-based alternatives cannot satisfy.
