AB  - Heterogeneous operational data ecosystems often contain multiple event sources, registry tables, implicit join paths, and domain specific field semantics, making natural language analytical access difficult for non technical stakeholders. This paper presents an LLM-based multi-agent architecture for schema-aware data mining in intelligent information systems. The proposed framework transforms natural language analytical requests into grounded and executable computations through semantic intent classification, adaptive orchestration, registry driven dataset routing, schema-constrained join derivation, retrieval supported schema grounding, and controlled Python code execution. Unlike direct question-answering or text-only retrieval approaches, the system explicitly separates query interpretation, source selection, schema grounding, executable computation, and stakeholder facing reporting. The workflow is implemented as a stateful multi-agent architecture in which each execution step produces inspectable intermediate artifacts, including selected data sources, candidate joins, grounding context, generated analytical plans, executed code, structured outputs, and final responses. This design supports inspectable data mining operations over heterogeneous structured sources and helps reduce structurally inconsistent analyses caused by invalid source selection, missing bridge tables, or incorrect aggregation levels. The framework is evaluated on a proprietary urban parking analytics case study involving heterogeneous operational sources, including payment transactions, control events, enforcement records, spatial registries, and IoT derived occupancy signals. A 45 query benchmark is used to assess functional robustness, structured analytical correctness against gold artifacts, and answer level decision support quality. Results show zero crash cases, 40 execution successful runs, an artifact level gold-success rate of 71%, an answer relevance of 0.780, and strong clarity in LLM-as-a-judge evaluation (4.62/5). These findings suggest that schemaaware orchestration can support bounded operational analytics in the considered urban parking case study, while also showing that compositional queries involving time series, grouped ratios, and matrix-style outputs require stronger validation mechanisms.
