AB  - Conversational AI has emerged as a transformative tool in retail banking, yet traditional text-based systems often fail to accommodate diverse user needs, particularly in spoken interactions. This paper introduces a voice-enabled Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework designed to enhance accessibility and inclusivity in retail banking services. Integrating Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR), semantic embedding with SentenceTransformers and FAISS indexing, Large Language Model (LLM)-based response generation via Cerebras, and Text-to-Speech (TTS) synthesis, the proposed modular pipeline leverages the Bitext Retail Banking LLM Chatbot Training Dataset to ensure domain-specific, compliant, and contextually accurate responses. A comparative evaluation across six configurations—varying embedding models, vector databases, and augmentation frameworks—assessed performance on subsets of 10,30, and 50 samples using metrics such as intent accuracy, entity F1-score, ROUGE, BLEU, relevance, latency, and robustness. Results demonstrate that the SentenceTransformer+FAISS+LangChain configuration achieves superior performance, with intent accuracy up to 0.89, ROUGE-1 scores of 0.91, and latency below 0.71 seconds on larger datasets, outperforming baselines in semantic fidelity and multi-turn dialogue coherence. This framework advances voice-driven banking assistants by mitigating hallucinations, optimizing real-time interaction, and promoting regulatory compliance, thereby fostering greater user trust and operational efficiency in financial services.
