AB  - Clinical diagnostic reasoning is a core medical competency, but traditional training is constrained by limited exposure to diverse real-world cases. On the other hand, although well known for question answering, large language models (LLMs) often fail as realistic training simulators due to premature diagnosis revelation or unnatural medical jargon. Therefore, we systematically analyze these limitations and construct Lavender Teeducation (LATEE), an intelligent tutoring system, for simulation-based clinical training. Using the MIMIC-IV database as the primary data source, narratives from the History of Present Illness were transformed into natural conversational dialogues to construct a diverse clinical case repository. The proposed system then integrates two core agents: (1) a High-Fidelity Virtual Patient, fine-tuned from Llama-3.1-8B using Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation (QLoRA) to simulate layperson behavior, and (2) a Pedagogical AI Assistant using Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and an 8B-parameter LLaMA model retrieving knowledge via internal protocols such as American Academy of Family Physicians and Nguyen Tri Phuong Hospital, governed by a sixlayer prompt for safety. Applying the Multi-Judge Triangulation framework with three automated evaluators yielded consistently favorable outcomes across both components. As a result, our study sheds light on the optimal practices to repurpose general-purpose LLMs for specialized medical education tasks. By synergizing high-fidelity simulation with intelligent tutoring, the system extends clinical insight training beyond physical boundaries and resource constraints, effectively enabling clinical diagnostic reasoning practice and ensuring scalable benefits for medical education.
ER  - 

