AB  - Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) demonstrate strong performance across a wide range of tasks, yet their effectiveness remains limited in low-resource and domainspecific contexts where annotated data are scarce. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) partially mitigates this issue by providing external knowledge, but its reliance on retrieval only during inference restricts domain adaptation. To address this, we propose RAILL (Retrieval-Augmented Instruction Tuning for Low-resource Language Model Training), a unified framework that integrates retrieval into both training and inference. By exposing the model to domain-relevant documents during instruction tuning, RAILL enables contextual grounding and internalization of domain knowledge, enhancing adaptability under limited supervision. We validate RAILL in the education domain by developing Edu-LLaMA, a model trained on bilingual (Korean-English) educational materials. Edu-LLaMA achieves substantial improvements, including applications like question answering and text generation, producing outputs that are both factually consistent and pedagogically aligned. Further experiments in the biomedical domain confirm that RAILL generalizes effectively across specialized low-resource settings. Overall, RAILL provides a general and efficient framework for building domain-adapted LLMs that maintain reliability, factual grounding, and cross-lingual robustness even when domainspecific data are limited.
ER  - 

