AB  - Knowledge base question answering (KBQA) answers natural language questions by executing structured queries over a large knowledge base, returning verifiable answers and explicit reasoning traces. Classic approaches span knowledge retrieval methods and semantic parsing methods. Recent work reframes knowledge base question answering (KBQA) as a generate-then-retrieve framework: an instruction-tuned large language model first generates a logic form (S-expression), which is then grounded to entities and relations via unsupervised retrieval and executed as a SPARQL query, yielding interpretable answers and strong results on knowledge base QA benchmarks. Building on this paradigm, we present RKBQA, a plug-and-play revise-once module inserted between logic form generation and retrieval. RKBQA decides when the top predicted logic form is already reliable and when it should be minimally revised. It first uses relation-level priors to skip unnecessary revisions, and only when needed invokes a chain-of-thought reviser that proposes edits to the initial logic form. The original prediction is kept as a fallback, and downstream components select entities and relations and execute the first grounded query that returns a non-empty answer. Experiments on WebQSP and ComplexWebQuestions (CWQ) show that RKBQA consistently improves F1 and exact-match accuracy over a strong generate-then-retrieve baseline. We also release an out-of-sample revision set and a CoT-based revision dataset pairing questions and predicted logic forms with machine-audited minimal edits, along with code, to facilitate reproducibility. Code and revision datasets are available at https://github.com/WenboHuang3/RKBQA
ER  - 

