AB  - Emergency operations in power grids require rapid interpretation of heterogeneous, time-varying information under dynamic and high-stakes conditions. Large Language Model (LLM) based digital assistants show promise for supporting situational awareness, but evaluating them safely under realistic emergency conditions remains challenging. We present Situationally Aware Assistant for Power Grid (SAAP-GRID), a simulation-grounded and context-aware orchestrator, along with an evaluation framework specifically in wildfire-related emergency scenarios relevant to power-grid operations. The developed framework uses physics-based simulations as controlled proxies for emergency conditions and benchmarks models through grounded question-answering tasks that require reasoning over time-varying system states. We instantiate the framework using a fire simulation engine and evaluate seven LLMs across factual retrieval, computational reasoning, trend analysis, comparative reasoning, temporal reasoning, and robustness tasks. Importantly, wildfire simulation outputs are treated as a dynamic hazard layer rather than as the final analytical target; the objective is to assess whether LLMs can translate evolving hazard conditions into power-grid-relevant situational awareness. We observe consistent gaps across computational, comparative, and temporal queries, indicating limitations in multi-step and spatio-temporal reasoning. While robustness is high for certain models, reliability remains inconsistent under complex scenarios. We also compare two external-grounding strategies, tool calling and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to assess their benefits in the studied emergency scenarios. Quantitatively, the best-performing model achieved perfect accuracy on factual, comparative, and temporal retrieval tasks, while computational reasoning remained the most challenging category (80%). External grounding through tool calling matched the strong direct-prompting baseline accuracy (93%) while improving robustness from 92% to 97%, whereas chain-of-thought prompting reduced overall accuracy to 85%, highlighting that structured grounding rather than unstructured reasoning improves LLM reliability in emergency power-grid scenarios. Overall, SAAP-GRID provides a scalable pre-deployment evaluation framework for assessing the performance and robustness of LLM-based assistants in safety-critical power-grid contexts.
ER  - 

