AB  - Large language models are increasingly used in financial question answering and advisory systems, but their responses may contain hallucinated facts, incorrect numerical claims, outdated information or cross-turn contradictions. This paper presents the Financial Hallucination Reliability Index (FHRI), a scenario-aware framework for detecting unreliable financial LLM responses. FHRI combines five complementary reliability signals, namely grounding, numerical or directional consistency, temporal validity, citation quality and entropy-based confidence, and augments them with natural language inference-based contradiction detection. To account for differences across financial query types, FHRI uses scenario-specific thresholds instead of a single global decision boundary. Experiments on a 10,000-sample annotated dataset covering 10 financial scenarios and three classes show that scenario-specific FHRI achieves 95.2% accuracy and 0.8999 Macro F1, outperforming both a global-threshold FHRI baseline and an entropyonly baseline. The results show that multi-signal, scenarioaware reliability scoring can improve the trustworthiness of LLM-generated financial responses.
