AB  - With the additional growth in natural comprehension, rationality, and generation for large language models (llMs), the inefficiencies, non-scaling, and non-coordination issues for the case with multiple agents grow more severe. In this work, we use an organizational structure-inspired collaboration model between Manager and Executor for our system. Our system offloads global planning and the sub-task decomposition for Manager agents with large capacities and reserve the rest for Executor agents with lightweights for taskexecution. A Model Context Protocol (MCP), a format-based communication protocol, is specified for standardized inter-agent coordination. To assess our model, we experiment with our model under three normal NLP tasks: long document summarization, multi-turn question answering, and structured table generation. In assist, we use a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) module as an auxiliary accelerator. Experimental results demonstrate significant gains for our model for the measures of task-execution efficiency, memory consumption, and coordination quality under the single-agent baseline measure. Comparison with the recent systems like HALO and Agent Orchestra reveals the superiority for our system with static task decomposition, structure-based communication, and role partitioning.
