AB  - As Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems increasingly support human decision-making, the design of explanations that balance technical fidelity and human understandability becomes critical. Traditional Explainable AI (XAI) methods, such as SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), provide faithful feature attributions but are often difficult for laypeople to interpret, whereas Large Language Model (LLM)-generated explanations are fluent and intuitive, yet may lack grounding in the model’s true reasoning. Because explanation design directly shapes decision performance, explanation satisfaction, understandability, decision certainty, trust, and perceived workload, evaluating these human-centered outcomes is essential. To address this tension, we propose a modular explanation framework that integrates multiple human-centric strategies—including simple textual, detailed, expert SHAP-based, and counterfactual explanations—mediated by an LLM-based decision agent that adaptively selects the most suitable explanation based on task, user, and context. We evaluate this approach in a preliminary user study with 50 participants who classified news articles as real or fake, comparing traditional SHAP explanations with our framework. Results show that our proposed explanation framework improves human decision performance by 6.1% relative to SHAP-based explanations. In addition, our framework reduced perceived workload across multiple dimensions, with mental demand declining significantly. While these trends suggest promising potential for improving the explainability of conversational agents, further validation is needed to establish statistical robustness. Our findings highlight opportunities for future research to refine and expand upon faithful model explanations and usable, human-centered explanations in conversational agents.
ER  - 

