AB  - Structured artifacts in safety- and compliance-critical engineering encode meaning through both instance configurations and schema-scoped normative semantics (definitions, constraints, and guidelines). While long-context LLM prompting and generic RAG can retrieve supporting text, they often treat all evidence as untyped chunks and provide limited traceability for norm-grounded decisions. We propose a traceable QA framework that (i) represents a document as an instance graph (ABox) and a knowledge-augmented meta-model ontology (TBox+K) with typed, schema-scoped norm objects, (ii) performs schema-constrained meta-model linking to produce a replayable retrieval plan, (iii) retrieves evidence through separate fact and norm channels under an explicit scope filter, and (iv) generates answers as atomic claims with claim-level dual citations to retrieved facts and norms. A lightweight validator enforces index-range and existence constraints and maps citation indices to stable evidence identifiers for downstream verification. Beyond correctness, we evaluate attribution faithfulness with semantic perturbations that edit selected normative objects and test whether conclusions and norm citations track normative changes.
ER  - 

