AB  - With the widespread use of multimodal data-such as images, videos, audio, and text-in real-world scenarios, efficiently and accurately extracting high-level semantic information has become a key challenge in artificial intelligence research. In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated powerful capabilities in semantic understanding and generation, offering a new paradigm for multimodal semantic extraction. In this paper, we propose an abstractive description-based approach grounded in large language models. By transforming multimodal inputs into natural language descriptions and leveraging LLMs for semantic abstraction and structured output, the proposed method enables unified semantic representations of multimodal content. This approach not only preserves the rich information contained in the original data but also enhances the interpretability and generalization ability of downstream tasks, such as retrieval, question answering, and reasoning. We review the dominant technical paradigms in the literature, present our proposed framework, and discuss promising directions for future research.
